---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DatumDemo from '../../components/DatumDemo.astro';
import exampleCode from '../../examples/charts-basic.js?raw';
---

<DocsLayout
    title="Charts"
    description="Learn how Datum charts provide the shared surface for composable visualization layers."
>
    <h1>Charts</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        A Datum chart provides the shared drawing surface, coordinate
        system and configuration used by one or more visualization layers.
    </p>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Creating a chart</h2>

        <p>
            Create a chart with <code>Datum.chart()</code>. The
            <code>target</code> option identifies the element where the
            chart will be rendered, while <code>datum</code> contains
            the visualization layers that make up the chart.
        </p>

        <DatumDemo
            id="charts-basic"
            title="Basic chart"
            description="Area, line and dot layers sharing a single chart."
            code={exampleCode}
        />
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Target</h2>

        <p>
            The <code>target</code> option can be a CSS selector or
            an existing DOM element.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',
    datum: [...]
});`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            Datum creates its chart container and SVG inside the
            target element.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Layers</h2>

        <p>
            The <code>datum</code> option is an array of visualization
            layers. Multiple layers can share the same chart surface
            and coordinate system.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`datum: [
    Datum.area(values, {...}),
    Datum.line(values, {...}),
    Datum.dot(values, {...})
]`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            This composition model allows different visual
            representations of the same or related data to be
            combined into a single chart.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Size</h2>

        <p>
            Use <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> to control
            the chart dimensions.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',
    width: 800,
    height: 400,
    datum: [...]
});`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            When a numeric width is not supplied, Datum measures the
            available width of the chart container. The default
            fallback width is 600 pixels and the default height is
            360 pixels.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Chart text</h2>

        <p>
            Charts can include a title, subtitle and caption.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',

    title: 'Weekly activity',
    subtitle: 'Sessions over the last seven days',
    caption: 'Example data',

    datum: [...]
});`}</code></pre>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Automatic layout</h2>

        <p>
            Datum calculates the chart plot area from the data and
            configured layers. Axis spacing and padding are adjusted
            automatically to accommodate labels, ticks and legends.
        </p>

        <p>
            Bar, histogram, candlestick and heatmap layers use
            band-based horizontal positioning, while continuous
            layers such as lines and areas are positioned across the
            available plot width.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Configuration</h2>

        <p>
            Set the chart-level <code>resize: true</code> option to redraw when
            its container width changes. The configured height stays fixed
            (360 by default). Updates are batched per animation frame and ignore
            subpixel width changes. Entrance animations run only on initial
            render, and legend selections survive resizing. Call
            <code>chart.destroy()</code> to disconnect the observer and remove
            a responsive chart when it is no longer needed.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`const chart = Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',
    height: 380,
    resize: true,
    datum: [Datum.line(values)]
});`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            Set <code>config.xAxis.min</code> and <code>config.xAxis.max</code>
            to limit the X-axis. Numeric X categories and scatter charts use
            numeric values; text categories use zero-based category positions.
            Either bound may be omitted for automatic scaling. Bounds must be
            finite numbers, with min less than max. Marks outside the plot are
            clipped. Pie charts do not use these bounds.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`config: {
    xAxis: { min: 1, max: 20 },
    yAxis: { min: 0, max: 100 }
}`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            Set <code>config.yAxis.min</code> and <code>config.yAxis.max</code>
            to fix the numeric Y-axis bounds. Either bound may be omitted to
            retain automatic scaling for that side. Explicit bounds override
            padding and the automatic zero baseline; marks outside the bounds
            are clipped to the plot. These options apply to Cartesian charts,
            rather than pie charts or categorical heatmap axes.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',
    config: {
        yAxis: { min: 0, max: 100 }
    },
    datum: [Datum.line(values)]
});`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            Bounds must be finite numbers, with <code>min</code> less than
            <code>max</code> when both are supplied.
        </p>

        <p>
            Additional chart behavior is configured through the
            <code>config</code> object.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',

    config: {
        padding: {
            top: 24,
            right: 24,
            bottom: 36,
            left: 36
        },

        xAxis: {
            labelAngle: 0,
            interval: 1
        }
    },

    datum: [...]
});`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            Datum automatically calculates padding when individual
            values are not supplied.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Animation</h2>

        <p>
            Set <code>animated</code> to <code>true</code> to enable
            chart animation.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',
    animated: true,
    animationDelay: '40ms',
    datum: [...]
});`}</code></pre>

        <p>
            <code>animationDelay</code> accepts a millisecond value
            such as <code>'40ms'</code> or <code>'200ms'</code>.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Tooltips</h2>

        <p>
            Tooltips are enabled by default. Set
            <code>tooltip</code> to <code>false</code> when a chart
            should not display interactive tooltip information.
        </p>

        <pre><code>{`Datum.chart({
    target: '#chart',
    tooltip: false,
    datum: [...]
});`}</code></pre>
    </section>

</DocsLayout>


<script>
    import '../../examples/charts-basic.js';
</script>
